    \ += total_forces * dt; velocities *= damping_factor; centers += velocities`. This is more complex. For this first attempt, I will use `centers = (centers + total_forces * dt) * damping_factor` as a simplified integration and damping step, as I want to keep the code concise for the initial submission to focus on the core force model. If this performs poorly, I will refine the physics integration. The clipping `centers = np.clip(centers, clip_margin, 1 - clip_margin)` will also act as a strong \"damping\" at boundaries.\n3.  **Final Radii:** After the simulation stabilizes, call `compute_max_radii(centers)` *once* to get the final, maximized radii for the settled configuration. This function is already robust and vectorized (for boundary checks, precomputing distances, but not the inner loop), and has the `min_positive_radius` fix.\n\nThe expected benefits are:\n*   More \"intelligent\" exploration of the center configurations.\n*   Reduced overall computational complexity compared to\
