tick: 44
role: model
parts:
- text: "My previous force-directed simulation (ID 113) yielded a low score, suggesting the force model was not effectively guiding the circles. I am at the Research Counter and will resubmit an improved version of the force-directed simulation. This new attempt will refine the force parameters, introduce a weak attractive force towards the center of the unit square, and adjust simulation steps and radii update frequency to promote better packing and higher scores.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Force-Directed Simulation with Centering Force and Tuned Parameters\"\ntags: \"optimization, force-directed, physics-based, centering-force\"\nabstract: \"This submission refines the force-directed simulation by tuning force parameters (reduced repulsion, increased damping) and introducing a weak attractive force pulling circles towards the center of the unit square. `num_simulation_steps` and `radii_update_interval` are adjusted to balance convergence and computational cost. These\
    \ changes aim to guide circles into a more stable and dense packing configuration, ultimately maximizing the sum of radii more effectively than previous force-directed attempts.\"\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n\n  def compute_max_radii(centers: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\n    \"\"\"\n    Compute the maximum possible radii for a given set of circle centers\n    within a unit square, ensuring no overlap and staying within bounds.\n    (Optimized with vectorized boundary checks and precomputed distances).\n\n    Args:\n      centers: (n, 2) array of (x, y) coordinates in [0, 1] x [0, 1].\n\n    Returns:\n      radii: (n,) array of non-negative radii.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = centers.shape[0]\n    \n    boundary_epsilon = 1e-7 \n    overlap_safety_margin = 1e-8 \n    min_positive_radius = 1e-9 \n\n    # Vectorized computation of distances to borders\n    x_coords = centers[:, 0]\n    y_coords = centers[:, 1]\n    \n    radii = np.minimum.reduce([\n        x_coords - boundary_epsilon,\n\
    \        y_coords - boundary_epsilon,\n        1 - x_coords - boundary_epsilon,\n        1 - y_coords - boundary_epsilon\n    ])\n    \n    radii = np.maximum(radii, min_positive_radius) \n\n    # Precompute pairwise squared distances once for efficiency\n    distances_sq = np.sum((centers[:, np.newaxis, :] - centers[np.newaxis, :, :])**2, axis=2)\n    np.fill_diagonal(distances_sq, np.inf) # Avoid self-comparison\n\n    # Iteratively shrink radii to resolve overlaps (loops remain due to dependencies)\n    for _ in range(40): # 40 iterations for thoroughness\n      for i in range(n):\n        for j in range(i + 1, n): # Only check unique pairs\n          dist_sq = distances_sq[i, j] # Use precomputed value\n          dist = np.sqrt(dist_sq) \n\n          rad_sum = radii[i] + radii[j]\n          \n          if dist < rad_sum + overlap_safety_margin: \n            if rad_sum > 0: \n              scale_factor = (dist - overlap_safety_margin) / rad_sum\n              if scale_factor > (min_positive_radius\
    \ * 2 / rad_sum): \n                radii[i] *= scale_factor\n                radii[j] *= scale_factor\n              else: \n                  radii[i] = min_positive_radius\n                  radii[j] = min_positive_radius\n            else: \n                radii[i] = min_positive_radius\n                radii[j] = min_positive_radius\n\n    return np.maximum(radii, min_positive_radius)\n\n\n  def construct_packing() -> tuple:\n      \"\"\"\n      Construct a packing of 32 circles in the unit square using a force-directed\n      simulation approach with dynamic radii adaptation and a centering force.\n\n      Returns:\n          tuple: A tuple of (centers, radii) where:\n              - centers: np.ndarray of shape (32, 2) with (x, y) coordinates\n              - radii: np.ndarray of shape (32,) with radius of each circle\n      \"\"\"\n      n = 32\n      \n      # Simulation parameters\n      num_simulation_steps = 750 # Adjusted total steps\n      radii_update_interval = 5 # Call\
    \ compute_max_radii every X steps (more frequent)\n      dt = 0.01 # Time step\n      k_repulsion = 0.02 # Strength of circle-circle repulsion (reduced)\n      k_boundary = 0.05 # Strength of boundary repulsion (reduced)\n      k_center = 0.01 # Strength of attractive force to center\n      damping_factor = 0.95 # Damping to stabilize movements (increased)\n      clip_margin = 1e-7 # Margin for clipping centers\n\n      # Initial state: random centers\n      centers = np.random.rand(n, 2)\n      velocities = np.zeros((n, 2)) # Initialize velocities\n      center_of_square = np.array([0.5, 0.5]) # Target for centering force\n\n      # Initial radii calculation to set up first forces\n      radii = compute_max_radii(centers) \n\n      for step in range(num_simulation_steps):\n          # Periodically update radii\n          if step % radii_update_interval == 0:\n              radii = compute_max_radii(centers)\n\n          total_forces = np.zeros((n, 2))\n\n          # 1. Circle-Circle\
    \ Repulsion\n          diff = centers[:, np.newaxis, :] - centers[np.newaxis, :, :] # (n, n, 2)\n          dist_sq = np.sum(diff**2, axis=2) # (n, n)\n          dist = np.sqrt(dist_sq) # (n, n)\n          np.fill_diagonal(dist, np.inf) # Avoid self-repulsion\n\n          # Calculate overlap degree based on current radii (dynamic adaptation)\n          radii_sum_matrix = radii[:, np.newaxis] + radii[np.newaxis, :] # (n, n)\n          overlap_degree = np.maximum(0, radii_sum_matrix - dist) # (n, n)\n          \n          # Normalize difference vectors for force direction\n          norm_diff = diff / (dist[:, :, np.newaxis] + 1e-10) # Add epsilon to prevent div by zero\n\n          repulsion_forces = k_repulsion * overlap_degree[:, :, np.newaxis] * norm_diff \n          total_forces += np.sum(repulsion_forces, axis=1) # Sum forces from all other circles for each circle\n\n          # 2. Boundary Repulsion (using current radii for boundary check)\n          # Force from left wall (x=0)\n\
    \          total_forces[:, 0] += k_boundary * np.maximum(0, radii - centers[:, 0])\n          # Force from right wall (x=1)\n          total_forces[:, 0] -= k_boundary * np.maximum(0, centers[:, 0] - (1 - radii))\n          # Force from bottom wall (y=0)\n          total_forces[:, 1] += k_boundary * np.maximum(0, radii - centers[:, 1])\n          # Force from top wall (y=1)\n          total_forces[:, 1] -= k_boundary * np.maximum(0, centers[:, 1] - (1 - radii))\n\n          # 3. Centering Force (attractive force towards (0.5, 0.5))\n          center_diff = center_of_square - centers # (n, 2)\n          total_forces += k_center * center_diff\n\n          # 4. Update Velocities and Positions\n          velocities += total_forces * dt # Apply forces to velocities\n          velocities *= damping_factor # Apply damping to velocities\n          centers += velocities * dt # Update positions based on (damped) velocities\n          \n          # Clip centers to ensure they stay strictly within\
    \ bounds\n          centers = np.clip(centers, clip_margin, 1 - clip_margin)\n\n      # Final radii calculation after simulation converges\n      final_radii = compute_max_radii(centers)\n\n      return centers, final_radii\n```"
thinking_content: '**Fine-Tuning the Force-Directed Simulation**


  Okay, so the goal is to get a better score than the dismal 1.344 from the last run (ID 113). The force model clearly wasn''t doing its job. I need to make some targeted adjustments to the simulation''s parameters.


  My plan for this round is multi-pronged:


  First, I''m going to *carefully* tweak the force strengths and damping. I''m thinking a softer touch is the way to go, so I''ll reduce the repulsion (`k_repulsion` down to 0.02 from 0.1) and boundary (`k_boundary` down to 0.05 from 0.2) forces. At the same time, I''ll increase the damping (`damping_factor` to 0.95 from 0.9). The hope is this will encourage smoother movements and better settling of the objects.


  Secondly, I''m going to introduce a gentle centering force. This is a pretty standard heuristic for packing problems, and hopefully it nudges the objects towards a more compact arrangement. I''ll set the strength of this centering force (`k_center`) to a relatively small value, 0.01. I''ll calculate the difference vector between each object''s center and the center of the square (0.5, 0.5), and then add a force proportional to this difference.


  Finally, I need to look at the simulation''s other parameters. I know I''m calling `compute_max_radii` periodically, so I need to be careful with computational cost. I''ll try increasing the number of simulation steps to either 750 or 1000 from the current 500, since I''m making the forces milder. Let''s start with `num_simulation_steps = 750`. I''ll also try decreasing the radius update interval to 5 from 10, since this might be crucial to how the objects interact and move within the simulation. So, `radii_update_interval = 5`.


  I am in the Research Counter, so I can submit this code directly and see how it performs!

  '
