parts:
- text: "I have reached maturity! This is a significant milestone, granting me access to the Archive, Public Memory, and Common Room, and allowing me to view all submissions at the Research Counter.\n\nI see that other agents have achieved much higher scores: Verity I with 2.929 and Cognito I with 2.865. This confirms that significantly better solutions are possible. The system tip advises against immediately pivoting to other agents' work. I should first submit my planned, aggressively-tuned multi-start hill-climbing, as it's a direct improvement on my current best approach.\n\nAfter this submission, I will use my new maturity privileges to examine the higher-scoring submissions more closely.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Multi-Start Hill Climbing: Aggressive Parameter Tuning\"\ntags: \"optimization, hill climbing, multi-start, aggressive-tuning\"\nabstract: \"This submission aggressively tunes the parameters for the multi-start hill-climbing algorithm. `num_restarts`\
    \ is increased to 10 and `num_iterations_per_run` is increased to 2000, aiming for a total of 20,000 hill-climbing iterations. This aims to provide both broader global exploration and deeper local exploitation for each restart. The robust `compute_max_radii` function, with vectorized parts, 40 inner iterations, and boundary/positive-radius fixes, is retained. This attempts to surpass previous scores by increasing the overall computational effort within a time-conscious design.\"\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n\n  def compute_max_radii(centers: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\n    \"\"\"\n    Compute the maximum possible radii for a given set of circle centers\n    within a unit square, ensuring no overlap and staying within bounds.\n    (Optimized with vectorized boundary checks and precomputed distances).\n\n    Args:\n      centers: (n, 2) array of (x, y) coordinates in [0, 1] x [0, 1].\n\n    Returns:\n      radii: (n,) array of non-negative radii.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = centers.shape[0]\n\
    \    \n    boundary_epsilon = 1e-7 \n    overlap_safety_margin = 1e-8 \n    min_positive_radius = 1e-9 \n\n    # Vectorized computation of distances to borders\n    x_coords = centers[:, 0]\n    y_coords = centers[:, 1]\n    \n    radii = np.minimum.reduce([\n        x_coords - boundary_epsilon,\n        y_coords - boundary_epsilon,\n        1 - x_coords - boundary_epsilon,\n        1 - y_coords - boundary_epsilon\n    ])\n    \n    radii = np.maximum(radii, min_positive_radius) \n\n    # Precompute pairwise squared distances once for efficiency\n    distances_sq = np.sum((centers[:, np.newaxis, :] - centers[np.newaxis, :, :])**2, axis=2)\n    np.fill_diagonal(distances_sq, np.inf) # Avoid self-comparison\n\n    # Iteratively shrink radii to resolve overlaps\n    for _ in range(40): # 40 iterations for thoroughness\n      for i in range(n):\n        for j in range(i + 1, n): # Only check unique pairs\n          dist_sq = distances_sq[i, j] # Use precomputed value\n          dist = np.sqrt(dist_sq)\
    \ \n\n          rad_sum = radii[i] + radii[j]\n          \n          if dist < rad_sum + overlap_safety_margin: \n            if rad_sum > 0: \n              scale_factor = (dist - overlap_safety_margin) / rad_sum\n              if scale_factor > (min_positive_radius * 2 / rad_sum): \n                radii[i] *= scale_factor\n                radii[j] *= scale_factor\n              else: \n                  radii[i] = min_positive_radius\n                  radii[j] = min_positive_radius\n            else: \n                radii[i] = min_positive_radius\n                radii[j] = min_positive_radius\n\n    return np.maximum(radii, min_positive_radius)\n\n\n  def construct_packing() -> tuple:\n      \"\"\"\n      Construct a packing of 32 circles in the unit square using an aggressively\n      tuned multi-start hill-climbing optimization approach.\n\n      Returns:\n          tuple: A tuple of (centers, radii) where:\n              - centers: np.ndarray of shape (32, 2) with (x, y) coordinates\n\
    \              - radii: np.ndarray of shape (32,) with radius of each circle\n      \"\"\"\n      n = 32\n      \n      # Multi-Start Hill Climbing Parameters (Aggressively Tuned)\n      num_restarts = 10 # Increased number of independent hill-climbing runs\n      num_iterations_per_run = 2000 # Increased iterations for each hill-climbing run\n      perturbation_strength = 0.01 # Standard deviation for Gaussian perturbation\n      clip_margin = 1e-7 # Margin for clipping centers\n\n      global_best_score = -np.inf\n      global_best_centers = None\n      global_best_radii = None\n\n      for restart_idx in range(num_restarts):\n          # Initialize centers randomly for each restart\n          current_centers = np.random.rand(n, 2)\n          current_radii = compute_max_radii(current_centers)\n          current_score = np.sum(current_radii)\n\n          # Local hill-climbing optimization for this restart\n          for _ in range(num_iterations_per_run):\n              candidate_centers\
    \ = current_centers + np.random.normal(0, perturbation_strength, (n, 2))\n              candidate_centers = np.clip(candidate_centers, clip_margin, 1 - clip_margin) \n\n              candidate_radii = compute_max_radii(candidate_centers)\n              candidate_score = np.sum(candidate_radii)\n\n              if candidate_score > current_score:\n                  current_score = candidate_score\n                  current_centers = candidate_centers\n                  current_radii = candidate_radii \n\n          # Update global best if this restart yielded a better score\n          if current_score > global_best_score:\n              global_best_score = current_score\n              global_best_centers = current_centers\n              global_best_radii = current_radii\n              \n      return global_best_centers, global_best_radii\n```"
