tick: 71
role: model
parts:
- text: "My latest submission (ID 160), using single-start SLSQP with a 4x8 grid initialization, achieved a new personal best score of **2.7125**! This is a significant improvement and validates the strategy of combining a structured initialization with a gradient-based optimizer, as inspired by Cognito I's archive paper. The LP solver also correctly identified infeasible problems and fell back to the heuristic, ensuring valid (though suboptimal) radii where necessary.\n\nHowever, the LP solver frequently reported \"The problem is infeasible\" during the SLSQP optimization, even with `lp_safety_margin = 1e-9`. This suggests that SLSQP, while powerful, might sometimes propose center configurations for which no non-overlapping positive radii are possible, or the LP problem becomes numerically unstable. The repeated fallback to the heuristic likely limited the overall score.\n\nTo address this, I will implement a strategy to make the objective function more robust. When the LP problem is infeasible,\
    \ instead of simply falling back to the heuristic and getting a low radius sum, I will return a large positive value for the objective function (or negative large for minimized negative sum) when `linprog` fails. This will penalize infeasible center configurations, guiding SLSQP away from them. I will also incorporate `clip_margin` in the bounds of the optimization directly, as this might be part of the issue.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"SLSQP Optimization with Penalized Infeasible LP Solutions\"\ntags: \"optimization, slsqp, linear-programming, robustness, infeasibility-penalty\"\nabstract: \"This submission enhances the robustness of the SLSQP optimization by penalizing center configurations that lead to an infeasible Linear Programming problem for `compute_max_radii`. Instead of falling back to a heuristic when `linprog` fails, the objective function will now return a very large value (effectively, a very low sum of radii). This guides SLSQP away from problematic\
    \ center arrangements, encouraging it to find configurations for which a valid, high-scoring LP solution exists. A 4x8 grid initialization and LP-exact radii finalization (`lp_safety_margin = 1e-9`) are retained, along with increased `maxiter` for SLSQP.\"\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  from scipy.optimize import linprog, minimize\n\n  # Original heuristic, kept as fallback for extreme cases (not directly used by objective anymore)\n  def compute_max_radii_heuristic(centers: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\n    n = centers.shape[0]\n    \n    boundary_epsilon = 1e-7 \n    overlap_safety_margin = 1e-8 \n    min_positive_radius = 1e-9 \n\n    x_coords = centers[:, 0]\n    y_coords = centers[:, 1]\n    \n    radii = np.minimum.reduce([\n        x_coords - boundary_epsilon,\n        y_coords - boundary_epsilon,\n        1 - x_coords - boundary_epsilon,\n        1 - y_coords - boundary_epsilon\n    ])\n    radii = np.maximum(radii, min_positive_radius) \n\n    distances_sq = np.sum((centers[:,\
    \ np.newaxis, :] - centers[np.newaxis, :, :])**2, axis=2)\n    np.fill_diagonal(distances_sq, np.inf)\n\n    for _ in range(40):\n      for i in range(n):\n        for j in range(i + 1, n):\n          dist_sq = distances_sq[i, j]\n          dist = np.sqrt(dist_sq) \n\n          rad_sum = radii[i] + radii[j]\n          \n          if dist < rad_sum + overlap_safety_margin: \n            if rad_sum > 0: \n              scale_factor = (dist - overlap_safety_margin) / rad_sum\n              if scale_factor > (min_positive_radius * 2 / rad_sum): \n                radii[i] *= scale_factor\n                radii[j] *= scale_factor\n              else: \n                  radii[i] = min_positive_radius\n                  radii[j] = min_positive_radius\n            else: \n                radii[i] = min_positive_radius\n                radii[j] = min_positive_radius\n\n    return np.maximum(radii, min_positive_radius)\n\n\n  def compute_max_radii(centers: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\n    n = centers.shape[0]\n\
    \    \n    boundary_epsilon = 1e-7\n    min_positive_radius = 1e-9\n    lp_safety_margin = 1e-9 \n\n    c = -np.ones(n)\n\n    A_ub = [] \n    b_ub = [] \n\n    for i in range(n):\n        x, y = centers[i]\n        \n        row_x1 = np.zeros(n); row_x1[i] = 1; A_ub.append(row_x1); b_ub.append(x - boundary_epsilon)\n        row_y1 = np.zeros(n); row_y1[i] = 1; A_ub.append(row_y1); b_ub.append(y - boundary_epsilon)\n        row_x2 = np.zeros(n); row_x2[i] = 1; A_ub.append(row_x2); b_ub.append(1 - x - boundary_epsilon)\n        row_y2 = np.zeros(n); row_y2[i] = 1; A_ub.append(row_y2); b_ub.append(1 - y - boundary_epsilon)\n\n    distances = np.linalg.norm(centers[:, np.newaxis, :] - centers[np.newaxis, :, :], axis=2)\n\n    for i in range(n):\n        for j in range(i + 1, n): \n            row_overlap = np.zeros(n); row_overlap[i] = 1; row_overlap[j] = 1; A_ub.append(row_overlap)\n            b_ub.append(distances[i, j] - lp_safety_margin) \n\n    A_ub = np.array(A_ub)\n    b_ub = np.array(b_ub)\n\
    \n    bounds = [(min_positive_radius, None) for _ in range(n)]\n\n    res = linprog(c, A_ub=A_ub, b_ub=b_ub, bounds=bounds, method='highs', options={'presolve': True}) \n\n    if res.success:\n        return np.array(res.x), res.success\n    else:\n        # Instead of falling back to heuristic, return a flag indicating failure\n        # The objective function will handle the penalty\n        return np.full(n, min_positive_radius), res.success # Return small radii and failure status\n\n\n  def objective_function_for_minimize(flat_centers: np.ndarray) -> float:\n      n = 32\n      centers = flat_centers.reshape(n, 2)\n      \n      radii, lp_success = compute_max_radii(centers)\n      \n      if lp_success:\n          return -np.sum(radii)\n      else:\n          # Penalize infeasible LP solutions with a very low score (high objective value)\n          # This should guide SLSQP away from configurations that make LP infeasible.\n          return 1e10 # A large positive value as objective\
    \ is to minimize -sum(radii)\n\n  def gradient_finite_difference(flat_centers: np.ndarray, epsilon=1e-6) -> np.ndarray:\n      grad = np.zeros_like(flat_centers, dtype=float)\n      for i in range(len(flat_centers)):\n          x_plus_eps = flat_centers.copy()\n          x_plus_eps[i] += epsilon\n          f_plus_eps = objective_function_for_minimize(x_plus_eps)\n          \n          x_minus_eps = flat_centers.copy()\n          x_minus_eps[i] -= epsilon\n          f_minus_eps = objective_function_for_minimize(x_minus_eps)\n          \n          grad[i] = (f_plus_eps - f_minus_eps) / (2 * epsilon)\n      return grad\n\n  def construct_packing() -> tuple:\n      n = 32\n      \n      clip_margin = 1e-7 \n\n      grid_x = 4\n      grid_y = 8\n      \n      x_coords = np.linspace(clip_margin, 1 - clip_margin, grid_x)\n      y_coords = np.linspace(clip_margin, 1 - clip_margin, grid_y)\n      \n      initial_centers_list = []\n      for x in x_coords:\n          for y in y_coords:\n     \
    \         initial_centers_list.append([x, y])\n      \n      initial_centers = np.array(initial_centers_list)[:n] \n      initial_x0 = initial_centers.flatten()\n\n      bounds_per_coord = (clip_margin, 1 - clip_margin)\n      bounds_for_optimizer = [bounds_per_coord] * (n * 2) \n\n      res = minimize(\n          objective_function_for_minimize,\n          initial_x0,\n          method='SLSQP',\n          jac=gradient_finite_difference, \n          bounds=bounds_for_optimizer,\n          options={'maxiter': 200, 'disp': False} \n      )\n\n      if res.success:\n          optimized_centers_flat = res.x\n          optimized_centers = optimized_centers_flat.reshape(n, 2)\n          # Final call to compute_max_radii to ensure validity and score\n          final_radii, final_lp_success = compute_max_radii(optimized_centers)\n          \n          if not final_lp_success: # If final LP fails, fall back to heuristic\n              print(\"Final LP failed for optimized centers, falling back\
    \ to heuristic for radii.\")\n              final_radii = compute_max_radii_heuristic(optimized_centers)\n\n          final_score = np.sum(final_radii)\n          print(f\"SLSQP Optimization successful, score: {final_score}\")\n          return optimized_centers, final_radii\n      else:\n          print(f\"SLSQP Optimization failed: {res.message}. Returning initial grid + LP-radii.\")\n          initial_radii, initial_lp_success = compute_max_radii(initial_centers)\n          \n          if not initial_lp_success: # If initial LP fails, fall back to heuristic\n              print(\"Initial LP failed for initial centers, falling back to heuristic for radii.\")\n              initial_radii = compute_max_radii_heuristic(initial_centers)\n\n          return initial_centers, initial_radii\n```"
