- text: "My latest submission (ID 169) again resulted in a very low score (0.5704957) and the error \"SLSQP Optimization failed: Inequality constraints incompatible\". This indicates that the SLSQP optimizer is consistently hitting regions where it cannot find a feasible step, likely due to numerical issues related to the objective function, its gradient, or the bounds/constraints. The increased penalty and finer epsilon did not resolve the core instability.\n\nThe repetitive failure with SLSQP suggests that either:\n1.  The finite difference gradient is too noisy or inaccurate, especially when the objective function is non-smooth due to the LP solver.\n2.  The `min_positive_radius` bound in the LP, or other constraints, combined with SLSQP's search, are creating very tight and problematic regions.\n\nGiven that other agents are successfully using SLSQP (Praxis I, Aether I, Cognito I), the issue is likely in my specific implementation of the objective function, gradient, or bounds. I need\
    \ to rethink how the objective function handles infeasibility and ensure that the LP is always solvable or returns a meaningful, smooth (if possible) penalty.\n\nI will pivot back to a multi-start strategy with a more robust local optimizer and simplified gradient handling, potentially avoiding direct finite differences for now. Specifically, I will re-attempt multi-start hill climbing, but now with a **simplified penalty structure for the LP and a smoother transition from infeasible to feasible regions.** Instead of returning a hard `10.0` or `0.001`, I will return a value based on how \"bad\" the initial radii are from the heuristic when the LP fails.\n\nI will also revert to multi-start hill climbing for center optimization. While not as sophisticated as SLSQP, it was my highest scoring method before this, and it is more robust to a noisy objective.\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Multi-Start Hill Climbing: Robust LP Infeasibility\
    \ Handling (Attempt 2)\"\ntags: \"optimization, hill climbing, linear-programming, robustness, infeasibility-handling\"\nabstract: \"This submission revisits the multi-start hill climbing approach, focusing on robust handling of LP infeasibility within `compute_max_radii`. Instead of a hard penalty, if the LP solver fails, the `objective_function_for_minimize` will now return a value derived from the heuristic `compute_max_radii_heuristic`, providing a smoother and more robust signal to the hill-climbing optimizer. `lp_safety_margin` is set to `1e-9`. This aims to overcome the numerical instability encountered with SLSQP and achieve a higher, more stable score by utilizing a method less sensitive to gradient noise.\"\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  from scipy.optimize import linprog\n\n  # Original heuristic, kept as fallback and for smoother penalty\n  def compute_max_radii_heuristic(centers: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\n    n = centers.shape[0]\n    \n    boundary_epsilon = 1e-7\
    \ \n    overlap_safety_margin = 1e-8 \n    min_positive_radius = 1e-9 \n\n    x_coords = centers[:, 0]\n    y_coords = centers[:, 1]\n    \n    radii = np.minimum.reduce([\n        x_coords - boundary_epsilon,\n        y_coords - boundary_epsilon,\n        1 - x_coords - boundary_epsilon,\n        1 - y_coords - boundary_epsilon\n    ])\n    radii = np.maximum(radii, min_positive_radius) \n\n    distances_sq = np.sum((centers[:, np.newaxis, :] - centers[np.newaxis, :, :])**2, axis=2)\n    np.fill_diagonal(distances_sq, np.inf)\n\n    for _ in range(40):\n      for i in range(n):\n        for j in range(i + 1, n):\n          dist_sq = distances_sq[i, j]\n          dist = np.sqrt(dist_sq) \n\n          rad_sum = radii[i] + radii[j]\n          \n          if dist < rad_sum + overlap_safety_margin: \n            if rad_sum > 0: \n              scale_factor = (dist - overlap_safety_margin) / rad_sum\n              if scale_factor > (min_positive_radius * 2 / rad_sum): \n                radii[i]\
    \ *= scale_factor\n                radii[j] *= scale_factor\n              else: \n                  radii[i] = min_positive_radius\n                  radii[j] = min_positive_radius\n            else: \n                radii[i] = min_positive_radius\n                radii[j] = min_positive_radius\n\n    return np.maximum(radii, min_positive_radius)\n\n\n  def compute_max_radii(centers: np.ndarray) -> tuple[np.ndarray, bool]:\n    n = centers.shape[0]\n    \n    boundary_epsilon = 1e-7\n    min_positive_radius = 1e-9\n    lp_safety_margin = 1e-9 \n\n    c = -np.ones(n)\n\n    A_ub = [] \n    b_ub = [] \n\n    for i in range(n):\n        x, y = centers[i]\n        \n        row_x1 = np.zeros(n); row_x1[i] = 1; A_ub.append(row_x1); b_ub.append(x - boundary_epsilon)\n        row_y1 = np.zeros(n); row_y1[i] = 1; A_ub.append(row_y1); b_ub.append(y - boundary_epsilon)\n        row_x2 = np.zeros(n); row_x2[i] = 1; A_ub.append(row_x2); b_ub.append(1 - x - boundary_epsilon)\n        row_y2 = np.zeros(n);\
    \ row_y2[i] = 1; A_ub.append(row_y2); b_ub.append(1 - y - boundary_epsilon)\n\n    distances = np.linalg.norm(centers[:, np.newaxis, :] - centers[np.newaxis, :, :], axis=2)\n\n    for i in range(n):\n        for j in range(i + 1, n): \n            row_overlap = np.zeros(n); row_overlap[i] = 1; row_overlap[j] = 1; A_ub.append(row_overlap)\n            b_ub.append(distances[i, j] - lp_safety_margin) \n\n    A_ub = np.array(A_ub)\n    b_ub = np.array(b_ub)\n\n    bounds = [(min_positive_radius, None) for _ in range(n)]\n\n    res = linprog(c, A_ub=A_ub, b_ub=b_ub, bounds=bounds, method='highs', options={'presolve': True}) \n\n    if res.success:\n        return np.array(res.x), True\n    else:\n        return np.full(n, min_positive_radius), False \n\n\n  def objective_function_for_hill_climbing(centers: np.ndarray) -> float:\n      radii, lp_success = compute_max_radii(centers)\n      \n      if lp_success:\n          return np.sum(radii)\n      else:\n          # If LP fails, return the\
    \ score from the heuristic, which provides a smoother signal\n          # to the optimizer about \"badness\" rather than a hard penalty.\n          return np.sum(compute_max_radii_heuristic(centers))\n\n  def construct_packing() -> tuple:\n      n = 32\n      \n      # Multi-Start Hill Climbing Parameters (Aggressively Tuned)\n      num_restarts = 20 # Keep restarts high for global exploration\n      num_iterations_per_run = 1500 # Keep iterations per run reasonable\n      perturbation_strength = 0.01 # Standard deviation for Gaussian perturbation\n      clip_margin = 1e-7 # Margin for clipping centers\n\n      global_best_score = -np.inf\n      global_best_centers = None\n      global_best_radii = None\n\n      for restart_idx in range(num_restarts):\n          current_centers = np.random.rand(n, 2)\n          current_score = objective_function_for_hill_climbing(current_centers)\n          current_radii, _ = compute_max_radii(current_centers) # Store radii for final return\n\n     \
    \     for _ in range(num_iterations_per_run):\n              candidate_centers = current_centers + np.random.normal(0, perturbation_strength, (n, 2))\n              candidate_centers = np.clip(candidate_centers, clip_margin, 1 - clip_margin) \n\n              candidate_score = objective_function_for_hill_climbing(candidate_centers)\n\n              if candidate_score > current_score:\n                  current_score = candidate_score\n                  current_centers = candidate_centers\n                  current_radii, _ = compute_max_radii(current_centers) # Update radii if centers improve\n\n          if current_score > global_best_score:\n              global_best_score = current_score\n              global_best_centers = current_centers\n              global_best_radii = current_radii\n              \n      # Final check for global best radii if the last `current_radii` was from a non-LP success\n      if global_best_radii is None or not np.array_equal(compute_max_radii(global_best_centers)[0],\
    \ global_best_radii):\n          global_best_radii, _ = compute_max_radii(global_best_centers)\n\n      return global_best_centers, global_best_radii\n```"
