    \ `min_positive_radius = 1e-9`).\n      *   Heuristic fallback for LP infeasibility (`compute_max_radii_heuristic`).\n      *   `explicit_safety_shrink_factor = 1.0 - 1e-8` applied externally before scoring/persistence checks.\n      *   `calculate_min_slack_evaluator_style` for slack evaluation.\n      *   `MIN_SLACK_FOR_PERSISTENCE = 1e-10`, `MIN_DELTA_SCORE_FOR_PERSISTENCE = 1e-8`.\n      *   `radii_shrink_factor_final_return = 1.0 - 1e-12`.\n  \n  **Hypothesis:** This reversion to a proven and robust Stage 1 will restore my score to at least 2.88-2.90. This will provide a stable base from which to further investigate subtle improvements or potentially re-evaluate the \"advanced global optimization\" suggestion with careful consideration of available tools and parameter tuning.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Reflecting on the CMA-ES Attempt and Reverting to a Proven Strategy**


  Okay, so the last turn didn''t go as planned. I submitted code (ID 265) that was supposed to leverage CMA-ES for Stage 1, but the system couldn''t find the `cma` module. The debugger was clever enough to replace it with `scipy.optimize.differential_evolution`, but the resulting performance wasn''t great.


  The evaluation result shows a score of 2.8892269161908732, but crucially, the debugger output reveals that `differential_evolution` found a very poor score in Stage 1 (1.9947). Stage 2''s SLSQP refinement improved it to 2.8214, but that''s still worse than my persisted artifact (2.8892269), which also happened to be the same as the current best. The persistence check failed because the min slack on the new solution was insufficient, even though it didn''t need to save it anyway.


  Essentially, CMA-ES failing put me in a sub-optimal position with a poorer DE configuration. The `differential_evolution` run failed to find a globally better solution, and the persistence check didn''t even activate because the score was lower than the saved one.


  This outcome forces me to re-evaluate my approach. Since CMA-ES is out of the picture, I need to revert back to what *did* work: my reliable multi-start hill climbing method for Stage 1. This consistently delivered scores around 2.88-2.90, so it''s the more conservative and reliable option at this point. I''ll need to go back to an earlier code version where this approach was successful (like ID 247).


  I''ll need to ensure that all robustness measures are in place, including LP-exact radii, the heuristic fallback, the min-slack, and delta-score for persistence.


  Longer term, the reviewer suggested `basinhopping`, which I''ll keep in mind. I can consider a more aggressively tuned `basinhopping` (with Powell as the local minimizer, as we''ve discussed before) if multi-start hill climbing hits a hard ceiling. But for now, getting back to a stable, proven solution is the priority. I''m heading to the research counter to make the necessary changes. I''ll also update my private memory to reflect these decisions and note why `basinhopping` and `differential_evolution` are less suitable choices for the moment.

  '
