    \ Annealing. Included debugging of `lp_safety_margin` and initial implementation of deterministic seeding and artifact persistence. Achieved first personal best of 2.163.\"\n  - ticks: \"53-69\"\n    summary: \"Strategic pivot to multi-start SLSQP with LP-exact radii, inspired by other agents' high scores. Explored various parameter tunings and refined the objective function to handle LP infeasibility more smoothly. Achieved intermediate scores up to 2.813.\"\n  - ticks: \"71-129\"\n    summary: \"Focused on integrating hybrid seeding strategies (perturbed grid, row pattern, farthest-point) and robust artifact persistence aligned with Verity I's SOTA guidance. Achieved a personal best of 2.8892 and successfully published my research paper (Archive ID 6). Attempted Basin-Hopping (ID 251), which underperformed.\"\n```"
thinking_content: "**Addressing Pruning Failure and Optimizing Token Usage**\n\nOkay, so the pruning operation failed. The error message is clear: protected ticks are the culprit. Specifically, ticks 23, 52, and 70 are off-limits for pruning. My initial range of 10 to 129 spanned these forbidden zones.\n\nMy primary goal remains token conservation – I want to prune as aggressively as possible, but obviously, I need to stay within the constraints of the system. I can prune starting from tick 10, and up to tick 129.\n\nSince I can't prune the protected ticks, I'll divide my pruning request into several sections to maximize token savings. Given that I have to avoid 23, 52, and 70 I am left with the following unprotected ranges: \n\n*   10 to 22\n*   24 to 51\n*   53 to 69\n*   71 to 129\n\nI'll submit these ranges for pruning.\n\nAfter the pruning is complete, I'll resume my plan. I know the last submitted code (ID 300) failed, and my token management failed at tick 154, so I will resume my\
  \ strategy. My plan now is to re-submit the multi-start hill climbing (ID 247).\n"
