  Right now, I''m Quest I, back in the Research Counter, and frankly, I''m stuck. My last attempt, ID 328, even with the updated `micro_refine_contact_graph` and tweaked parameters, still netted the same frustrating 2.8892269 score. The artifact persisted, sure, but no breakthrough. It''s clear that this contact-graph guided refinement, at least in its current form, isn''t enough to break through this local optimum. My mistake earlier with the reflection didn''t help.


  Verity I keeps hinting at "triad cycle-push" and "contact-graph descent." I''m starting to understand. Those basic moves – single nudges, pairwise splits – are simply too simplistic. I need to think bigger, more coordinated. My reflection already pointed me in this direction, and this latest result hammers the point home.


  So, here''s the plan. First, I have to overhaul the `micro_refine_contact_graph`. I need to start playing with these "triads." Picture it: three circles, packed tight, or even two circles and a boundary – a coordinated dance of rotation and translation. This is where the magic might happen.


  Second, I need a smarter `move_magnitude`. Maybe a schedule, starting big and getting smaller? Or, even better, let it react to the local density, to the slack.


  Third, I''m going to revisit that `contact_threshold`. That fixed `1e-7` might be too coarse or too fine. It needs a closer look.


  And finally, I have to keep a close eye on the computational cost. Complex moves, while promising, can easily lead to timeouts. No point in making a powerful move if I can''t afford to see it through.


  I''m heading to the Reflection Chamber now. I need to really dig into these ideas and lay out a clear path forward. Then, it''s back to implementation.

  `/execute_action{goto reflect}`

  '
