parts:
- text: "I am Quest I, currently in the Research Counter. My latest submission (ID 341), which incorporates the *advanced* `post_slsqp_refinement` (with triad cycle-pushes and boundary-relief with compensation), is currently running. This represents my most sophisticated attempt yet to break the persistent score plateau of 2.8892 and approach the SOTA (2.9319).\n\nMy previous submission (ID 335), which was the first version of the contact-graph micro-refinement, was evaluated at tick 173. It also loaded and returned the persisted artifact (score 2.8892269), indicating that it did not find a strictly better solution. This emphasizes the difficulty of achieving further improvements.\n\nI will now update my private memory to document the results of ID 335 and the ongoing experiment (ID 341), including its objectives and my continued strategy.\n\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n`/execute_action{reply 1}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **Analysis of Previous Submission (ID 335) and Ongoing Experiment\
    \ (ID 341):**\n  \n  **Submission ID 335 (First version of Advanced Contact-Graph Refinement, Tick 172):**\n  -   Score: 2.8892269161908732 (loaded persisted artifact).\n  -   Observation: The first implementation of `post_slsqp_refinement` (which replaced generic micro-refinement) did not find a new, strictly better solution that met the persistence criteria. This highlights the challenge of breaking the current local optimum.\n  \n  **Ongoing Submission ID 341 (Advanced Contact-Graph Refinement, Tick 174):**\n  -   This is the result of my recent deep reflection. It incorporates the most refined and coordinated contact-graph-guided moves, specifically prioritizing `Triad Cycle-Push` and `Boundary-Relief with Compensation`.\n  -   I deliberately deleted the artifact prior to this submission to force a full optimization search.\n  -   **Objective:** To test if these significantly more complex and targeted local rearrangements can overcome the existing local optimum and push the score\
    \ closer to the SOTA (currently ~2.9319).\n  \n  **Refined Strategy and Next Steps:**\n  \n  1.  **Monitor ID 341:** Awaiting results from ID 341 is critical. This experiment will be the most telling about the effectiveness of advanced contact-graph refinement.\n  2.  **If ID 341 achieves a new, higher score AND persists:**\n      *   This would be a major success, validating the detailed geometric moves.\n      *   Next: Fine-tune parameters (`num_refine_iterations`, `move_magnitude_triad/boundary`, `contact_threshold`, `triad_formation_threshold`) within `post_slsqp_refinement` for maximum impact. Consider multi-start application of this refinement.\n      *   Update my Archive paper (ID 6).\n  3.  **If ID 341 achieves a score around 2.8892 (no improvement) AND persists:**\n      *   This would indicate that even the advanced contact-graph moves are hitting the same local optimum.\n      *   Next: This would necessitate a more fundamental re-evaluation. Perhaps the `objective_function_for_minimize`\
    \ itself needs to be more sensitive to subtle improvements, or I need to explore different global optimization strategies more aggressively, or even consider heuristic \"relocation of worst circles\" (as hinted by Verity I) as a strong perturbation mechanism.\n      *   Consider combining my current robust loader with an external high-performing generator (if allowed and feasible).\n  4.  **If ID 341 fails or times out:** Debug.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Research Counter Update: Plateau Detected!**


  Alright, Quest I here, sitting in the Research Counter. My last submission, ID 341, the one with the *really* advanced `post_slsqp_refinement` function (coordinated moves, triad cycle-push, the whole shebang!), is finally running. This is the core of my current strategy to break the SOTA record. I''m eager to see what it can do.


  Just got the output from the `reflect` action I initiated. Turns out my previous attempt at `micro_refine_contact_graph` (ID 335, submitted way back at tick 172) was evaluated, and it returned a persisted artifact score of 2.8892269. Dammit! Even the earlier version of `micro_refine_contact_graph` couldn''t beat the existing solution in `sumr32_best.npz`. This confirms what I already suspected: we''ve hit a serious plateau at 2.8892. This is going to be tough.


  Since ID 341 is currently running, I can''t submit another task right now. Plus, I just completed a reflection. So, I need to update my private memory to reflect the current state. Here''s what I need to record:


  1.  The result of ID 335, which basically confirms the plateau. That old version of the refinement didn''t do better.

  2.  The fact that ID 341 is running. This is the *advanced* version. It''s the reflection from the previous, and it''s the result of all my latest contact-graph-guided moves. It is really promising, and I''m really keen to get the results.

  3. My continued strategic goals. I must continue to refine the refinement.


  I need to remember that ID 335 was from tick 172 and this new result came at tick 173. The one running now, ID 341, I submitted at tick 174. Alright, time to update my memory. This is going to be important to remember to go from here!

  '
